# Every domain-event `aggregateType()` in the app, classified by whether the AGGREGATE denotes a NATURAL PERSON.
#
# The rule this file exists to enforce: an "aggregate id alone" payload is safe to put on a persisted
# transport if and only if the aggregate is not a natural person. When it is, the aggregate id IS the
# personal datum, so an empty payload proves nothing — and `messenger_messages` and `failed` are Doctrine
# tables with no TTL and no prune that no erasure path touches, so the id outlives the deletion the
# application confirmed to the subject.
#
# Format, one per line:
#   <AggregateType> => non-person
#   <AggregateType> => person
#   <AggregateType> => person :: <path of the ADR recording why it is queued anyway>
#
# The gate (PersistentTransportPolicyGateTest, run by `make php.lint.persistent-transport`) fails the build
# when a `person` aggregate is reachable from a `framework.messenger.routing` key or an `#[AsMessage]`
# attribute on a transport other than `sync`, and when an aggregate type declared in `src` has no line here.
#
# What it deliberately does not do, stated so nobody mistakes a green build for a proof:
#
#   - It cannot judge the classification. Writing `Iam.Identity => non-person` passes. That is what review
#     is for; the gate's job is to force the decision to be made in a diffable file.
#   - It classifies the AGGREGATE, not the payload. A non-person aggregate whose payload carries a person's
#     id — `Iam.Session`'s `userId`, `Iam.Invitation`'s `invitedUserId` — is out of its reach, and closing
#     that is a separate piece of work (FR9/G-4b in the GDPR-hardening epic).
#   - It says nothing about `event_store`. Every dispatched event is appended there with its real
#     `aggregate_id`, regardless of routing, and no erasure path touches it.
#   - `sync` is treated as the only non-persisted transport, in every environment. Under `when@test` the
#     others are in-memory too, but modelling that would only ever weaken the gate.

Backoffice.Bank => non-person
Backoffice.BankAccount => non-person
Iam.Identity => person
Iam.Invitation => non-person
Iam.Session => non-person
